WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.350
The Saw series started out as a fever dream
project for film students James Wan and Leigh

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:09.080
Whannell but has since developed into a whole
franchise of gore.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:14.430
The central villain Jigsaw initially presented
himself as a non-serial killer who just wanted

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.680
to force troubled people into appreciating
and fighting for their lives.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.470
But as the series went on, his games eventually
made it clear that he and his minions just

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.170
wanted to see people suffer.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.820
As a result, some installments definitely
turned out better than others.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.760
From worst to first, here's a ranking of all
the Saw movies so far.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:33.460
"Let the game begin."

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.750
Saw 3D: The Final Chapter

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:39.610
By the time the seventh piece of the Jigsaw
puzzle came into play, this series looked

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.420
almost nothing like the original.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.280
In the pic, Detective Hoffman was so busy
running around cleaning up his own messes

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.270
that the day's games seemed like an afterthought
meant only to follow formula.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.379
In fact, there were two major group traps
that were completely devoid of value to the

00:00:53.379 --> 00:00:54.560
overarching plot.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.950
Whoever thought it was necessary to have a
man rip all the skin of his back off in 3D

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.660
might just be sicker than Jigsaw.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.350
There were a couple of elements of redemption—like
Dr. Gordon making a long-overdue comeback

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:07.810
to the series to tie up a few loose limbs.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:11.950
And there was a kernel of something interesting
in play with the fake Jigsaw survivor at the

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:12.950
center.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.279
But the movie was frustratingly disjointed,
and Hoffman's Terminator-style raid on the

00:01:16.279 --> 00:01:21.000
police department laid waste to the entire
framework of Jigsaw's "legacy."

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.289
Saw III

00:01:22.289 --> 00:01:26.459
The third installment of the Saw series was
one of the most problematic for several reasons.

00:01:26.459 --> 00:01:30.340
For starters, the victims were completely
sympathetic and even less deserving of their

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.899
fates than anyone else.

00:01:31.899 --> 00:01:35.469
Submitting the father and mother of a drunk
driving victim to torture trials just because

00:01:35.469 --> 00:01:37.829
they're not grieving the way you might want
them to?

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:39.659
That's cruel, even for Jigsaw.

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:43.560
Even worse was the fact that the new traps
for this installment were unnecessarily gruesome,

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.989
to the point of being unwatchable at times.

00:01:45.989 --> 00:01:49.750
And let's not forget about the table saw brain
surgery we just had to watch unfold.

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:53.799
Sure, it was a clever turn of fortune that
the person who was really being tested was

00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:55.859
protege number one, Amanda.

00:01:55.859 --> 00:02:00.380
But the choice of victims here seemed to exhaust
the whole generous Grim Reaper identity Jigsaw

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.189
crafted for himself in the previous pics,
making Saw III among the series' slackest

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:06.759
chapters.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:08.369
Saw VI

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:13.260
Whereas Saw V managed to make Detective Hoffman
interesting, Saw VI did not.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.099
Since he'd already killed off Strahm, and
the spate of drug addicts and other officers

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:21.860
who'd run afoul of Jigsaw were dead as well,
Hoffman's victims list became pretty arbitrary

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.180
and ridiculous.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:27.800
"Do you like how brutality feels, Mark?"

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:32.060
This time, he picked on a pair of predatory
loan sharks and one particularly nasty insurance

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.290
adjuster who'd denied John Kramer coverage
for some experimental cancer treatments.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:40.430
Bearing in mind the fact that this movie was
released in the wake of the subprime mortgage

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:45.319
crisis, it's understandable why the filmmakers
might try to get political and punish greedy

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:46.319
people.

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:50.689
However, the plot was still flimsy and underexplored,
and seemed extra gruesome just for the sake

00:02:50.689 --> 00:02:51.730
of shock value.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:56.230
Combined with some terrible acting and forced
twists, it became stock shlock long before

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:57.390
the timer ran out.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:01.480
The redeeming factor of the movie that keeps
it from landing last on the list is that Hoffman's

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:06.079
voyeuristic copycat game in the beginning
was a pretty decent metaphor for the Saw series

00:03:06.079 --> 00:03:07.469
as a whole.

00:03:07.469 --> 00:03:08.609
Saw IV

00:03:08.609 --> 00:03:12.569
For the fourth installment, director Darren
Lynn Bousman decided to delve into John Kramer's

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:17.379
wretched rationale by bringing in his tragic
story with his wife into the picture, along

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:22.049
with another secondary psycho to fill in the
timeline gaps as John Kramer took his leave

00:03:22.049 --> 00:03:23.129
of the world.

00:03:23.129 --> 00:03:27.749
But the central player of the games, Lieutenant
Rigg, was wholly undeserving of his trial.

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:32.310
So what if he was passionate about his job
and missed out on a family vacation or two?

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:37.909
The punishment never really matched the crime
in this series, but especially so for Rigg.

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:41.920
That said, the clever culmination of events
at the end was a fresh surprise that saved

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:42.920
this story.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.670
The fact that Jigsaw was still in control
of his crew, even while lying cold on an autopsy

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:53.349
table, was delightfully bonkers and managed
to renew any fading interest in what was next.

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:54.780
Saw V

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.540
By the time we got to the fifth film, it was
Detective Hoffman who'd taken over Jigsaw's

00:03:58.540 --> 00:03:59.540
kill crew.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.629
He was sloppy enough to attract some heat
from on high in the form of FBI special agent

00:04:03.629 --> 00:04:04.629
Strahm.

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:09.560
Strahm was obsessively committed to the cause
of nailing his prime suspect, even after escaping

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.660
his own death trap by way of an ink pen tracheotomy.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:16.980
So, the game of cat and mouse that ensued
between them was pretty epic.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:22.060
Meanwhile, the games that were afoot in Saw
V were also a bit more precise and interesting.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:25.740
The victims of this round weren't just helplessly
being ripped to shreds but instead at least

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:30.270
pretended to use their brains before engaging
those mechanisms that would cause their eventual

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.270
demise.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:35.130
The gore of the games was mercifully dialed
down a notch, which gave more room for the

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:39.310
sound effects team to fill in the blanks,
rather than always leaning on graphic visuals

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:40.580
to get the job done.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:44.199
This film's return to a teamwork element in
the games was refreshing, and the passionate

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:49.180
pursuit of Strahm culminated in a crushing
twist ending that was as jaw-dropping as anything

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:52.190
that came before it or after.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:53.720
Saw II

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:57.490
Director Darren Lynn Bousman managed to avoid
the sophomore slump with the first sequel

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:58.490
to Saw.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:02.340
In fact, the film gamely expanded the world
that'd been established in the original and

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:06.130
offered a new vein of intrigue for the subsequent
sequels to float on.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:10.190
The backbone of the selected group of victims,
stuck in a dirty doom house where they're

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:14.740
racing against the clock to flesh out their
own shared histories was still there.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:19.729
But the movie managed to layer that element
with a compelling subplot about Jigsaw's arresting

00:05:19.729 --> 00:05:22.800
officer, Detective Matthews, and his missing
son.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.509
And on top of that, there was the surprise
return of Amanda, which answered the question

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:31.479
of what would actually happen to those few
who managed to survive Jigsaw's game.

00:05:31.479 --> 00:05:35.690
Unlike some of the other sequels, the entire
plot was pretty polished and managed to give

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:41.000
the series some staying power, so movie two
is the second-best of the series.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Saw

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.040
There was something very artful about Saw's
approach to the macabre that made the movie

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:47.949
such an indelible experience.

00:05:47.949 --> 00:05:52.160
The puzzle pieces of skin taken from victims'
bodies were meant to be symbols of what was

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.290
missing from people's lives, but they were
also a collective metaphor for the audience's

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:59.669
job of digesting everything that happened
in the film.

00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:03.509
The small-scale setting of two and a half
people locked in a bathroom playing a game

00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:06.620
of Clue for their lives was surprisingly effective.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:11.630
And so were the whirly-cam montage tricks
used to show off other terror traps.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:16.960
So, Saw opened the door to much more interest
in exploring the sick, sad world of Jigsaw,

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:22.280
especially after it ended on such a twist
that it was decidedly not "game over."

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:24.800
The movie was still far from perfect, of course.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:28.629
The film had some moments of lazy acting and
low-budget camera angling that are pretty

00:06:28.629 --> 00:06:30.409
cringeworthy upon review.

00:06:30.409 --> 00:06:35.229
But even if it didn't boast the slickest cinematography
known to Hollywood, Saw was still an original

00:06:35.229 --> 00:06:40.550
and very disturbing abstraction for the auteurs
to explore and was executed with impressive

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.430
efficiency and invention.

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:44.199
For those reasons, it's still the best.

00:06:44.199 --> 00:06:48.580
We'll just have to wait and see if the next
sequel, Jigsaw, manages to bring some life

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:50.400
back into the series, but until then …

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:52.780
"Game over."

00:06:52.789 --> 00:06:53.990
"Noooo!"

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:54.990
Thanks for watching!

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.289
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:57.289 --> 00:06:59.389
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

